# Real finding, confirmed this session: mkosi runs `systemctl preset-all`
# itself as one of its own final build steps -- a plain `systemctl
# enable` in a postinst script gets silently undone by that (confirmed:
# "Removed '/buildroot/etc/.../slfhst-install.service'" in a real build
# log). A preset file is what actually survives, same lesson already
# learned for the appliance build's own units -- consistent mechanism,
# not a special case.
enable slfhst-install.service

# Base systemd's own systemd-firstboot.service runs its interactive
# "Initial Setup" wizard (timezone/root password prompts) on tty1 before
# slfhst-install.service gets a chance to -- this project's own installer
# already handles all of that through its own flow, so Fedora's generic
# one is pure noise here, not a fallback worth keeping.
#
# A `disable` preset line here does NOT stop it, confirmed by real
# boot-testing (both under QEMU and on real hardware -- it still ran):
# systemd-firstboot.service is enabled via a symlink shipped directly by
# the systemd package itself
# (usr/lib/systemd/system/sysinit.target.wants/systemd-firstboot.service),
# not via a preset-controlled /etc/systemd/system/*.wants/ entry --
# `systemctl preset-all` only ever writes/removes the latter, so it can
# never undo a package's own static Wants symlink. The actual fix is a
# real mask: mkosi.extra/etc/systemd/system/systemd-firstboot.service is
# a symlink to /dev/null (same as `systemctl mask` would create), which
# takes precedence over any Wants symlink regardless of where it lives.
# Kept here anyway since it's harmless and correct in spirit, just not
# what actually does the job.
disable systemd-firstboot.service
